WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:09.792
This is Chapter Two, Episode Five: local self-government in the municipalities and regions.

3
00:00:10.242 --> 00:00:13.554
Democracy does not only live in Christiansborg.

4
00:00:13.554 --> 00:00:17.562
Local self-government is a cornerstone of Danish democracy.

5
00:00:17.562 --> 00:00:23.322
It has deep roots in history that local communities solve many of society's common tasks.

6
00:00:23.322 --> 00:00:31.770
Denmark is among the countries in Europe that have placed the most tasks with local and regional government, in municipalities and regions.

7
00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:38.706
The tasks of Danish municipalities in particular have grown strongly as the welfare society has developed.

8
00:00:38.706 --> 00:00:43.818
Today municipalities and regions solve a wide range of the public sector's tasks.

9
00:00:43.818 --> 00:00:47.826
In that way they are an important foundation for the welfare society.

10
00:00:48.376 --> 00:00:54.189
Local self-government is a form of close-to-the-people democracy, a nærdemokrati.

11
00:00:54.139 --> 00:01:00.714
It gives the individual citizen the opportunity to influence the conditions that are close to citizens' everyday lives.

12
00:01:00.714 --> 00:01:03.676
That can be in the municipality or in the region.

13
00:01:03.676 --> 00:01:08.601
It happens, among other things, through municipal and regional elections.

14
00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:17.124
Alongside national democracy in the Folketing there is thus also local and regional democracy in the country's municipalities and regions.

15
00:01:17.674 --> 00:01:22.962
The municipalities' right to manage their own affairs independently is in the constitution.

16
00:01:22.912 --> 00:01:28.674
But there is not much else in it about the municipalities, and nothing about which tasks they should solve.

17
00:01:28.674 --> 00:01:35.061
It is the Folketing that sets the framework for local self-government and decides the municipalities' tasks.

18
00:01:35.061 --> 00:01:38.958
The state supervises how the municipalities solve their tasks.

19
00:01:39.508 --> 00:01:49.158
The idea behind local self-government is that public tasks that affect individual citizens' daily lives should be solved as close to the citizens as possible.

20
00:01:49.108 --> 00:01:53.283
This is often called the proximity principle, nærhedsprincippet.

21
00:01:53.283 --> 00:02:02.096
There is also a tradition that municipalities, as far as possible, should themselves be able to decide how they will solve and finance the local tasks.

22
00:02:02.096 --> 00:02:08.808
Often the municipalities get the chance to solve public tasks in the way that best suits local needs and wishes.

23
00:02:08.808 --> 00:02:11.958
Municipalities also have the right to levy tax.

24
00:02:11.958 --> 00:02:16.392
In that way they themselves help to raise most of the money to solve their tasks.

25
00:02:16.942 --> 00:02:22.217
But there is a big difference between the populations living in the individual municipalities.

26
00:02:22.167 --> 00:02:28.379
Some municipalities have many more citizens with high incomes than others, and thus a better tax base.

27
00:02:28.379 --> 00:02:39.292
Other municipalities have more citizens on transfer income, for example cash benefit, unemployment benefit or early retirement pension, and thus higher expenses.

28
00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:52.074
To make sure there is money for a reasonably uniform welfare society all over Denmark, there is therefore a series of municipal equalisation schemes and state grants that move a lot of money between the municipalities.

29
00:02:52.624 --> 00:02:58.661
Besides this, it is the Folketing and the government that set the conditions for the municipalities' work.

30
00:02:58.611 --> 00:03:03.974
And the relationship between state and municipalities is sometimes marked by conflict.

31
00:03:03.974 --> 00:03:15.649
The Folketing and the government are interested in the municipalities following national policy, and in all municipalities in the country giving citizens roughly the same offers and conditions.

32
00:03:15.649 --> 00:03:23.136
In contrast, some municipalities feel from time to time that the state interferes too much in local self-government.

33
00:03:23.136 --> 00:03:29.556
They think the municipalities are deprived of the opportunity to decide for themselves how they want to solve the tasks.

34
00:03:30.106 --> 00:03:33.418
Danish local government is constantly developing.

35
00:03:33.368 --> 00:03:39.181
On the first of January, 2007, a larger structural reform came into force.

36
00:03:39.181 --> 00:03:49.756
From that day, 271 municipalities were merged into 98, and the former 14 counties, amter, were replaced by 5 regions.

37
00:03:49.756 --> 00:03:55.931
The distribution of tasks between the state, the municipalities and the regions was also changed.

38
00:03:55.931 --> 00:03:59.818
The state and the municipalities can themselves levy taxes.

39
00:03:59.818 --> 00:04:11.214
But unlike the old counties, the regions do not have the possibility of levying tax themselves, but instead receive money to cover their expenses from the state and the municipalities.

40
00:04:11.764 --> 00:04:17.764
Denmark thus consists of 98 municipalities and is divided into five regions.

41
00:04:17.714 --> 00:04:22.901
Region Hovedstaden, the Capital Region, with 29 municipalities.

42
00:04:22.901 --> 00:04:27.239
Region Sjælland, Zealand, with 17 municipalities.

43
00:04:27.239 --> 00:04:31.951
Region Syddanmark, Southern Denmark, with 22 municipalities.

44
00:04:31.951 --> 00:04:37.026
Region Midtjylland, Central Denmark, with 19 municipalities.

45
00:04:37.026 --> 00:04:41.889
And Region Nordjylland, North Denmark, with 11 municipalities.

46
00:04:41.889 --> 00:04:48.451
Region Hovedstaden and Region Sjælland will be merged on the first of January, 2027.

47
00:04:48.451 --> 00:04:53.784
The new, merged region is to be called Region Østdanmark, Eastern Denmark.

48
00:04:54.334 --> 00:04:56.171
Now, who leads them?

49
00:04:56.121 --> 00:05:04.996
Danish municipalities are led by elected municipal councils, kommunalbestyrelser, which are often called city councils, byråd.

50
00:05:04.996 --> 00:05:13.909
The chair of the municipal council is called the mayor, borgmester, and is elected by the municipal council right after the municipal election.

51
00:05:13.909 --> 00:05:21.509
In the same way, each region is led by a regional council, a regionsråd, which elects a chair itself.

52
00:05:21.509 --> 00:05:27.546
Members of both municipal councils and regional councils are elected for four years at a time.

53
00:05:27.546 --> 00:05:36.426
The election is held all over the country every four years in November, and the municipalities and regions cannot choose to hold elections at other times.

54
00:05:36.976 --> 00:05:42.026
Every adult citizen has the right to take part in municipal and regional elections.

55
00:05:41.976 --> 00:05:47.701
You just have to be eighteen years old and have a permanent residence in the municipality or region.

56
00:05:47.701 --> 00:05:53.101
So you do not need to have Danish citizenship to take part in municipal and regional elections.

57
00:05:53.101 --> 00:06:04.013
It is enough that you are either a citizen of another EU country, Iceland or Norway, or that you have lived permanently in Denmark without interruption for the last four years before election day.

58
00:06:04.013 --> 00:06:12.876
All citizens who have the right to vote in municipal and regional elections can, as a main rule, also stand as candidates in these elections.

59
00:06:13.426 --> 00:06:22.376
Most nationwide political parties also stand in the local elections in municipalities and regions, but other parties and groups can also stand.

60
00:06:22.326 --> 00:06:30.513
Local questions often make local citizens' groups stand on their own candidate lists, the so-called local lists, lokallister.

61
00:06:30.513 --> 00:06:34.902
The local lists often fight for quite specific local political goals.

62
00:06:35.452 --> 00:06:43.102
The latest election to municipal councils and regional councils was held on the eighteenth of November, 2025.

63
00:06:43.052 --> 00:07:02.214
As a result of this election, the mayoral posts in the country's municipalities are distributed among the different nationwide parties like this: Venstre 42, the Social Democrats 26, the Conservative People's Party 20, SF 5, Liberal Alliance 2 and the Social Liberal Party 1.

64
00:07:02.214 --> 00:07:08.239
These parties thus hold the mayoral post in 96 of the country's 98 municipalities.

65
00:07:08.239 --> 00:07:23.077
In addition, Tønder Municipality has a mayor from the Schleswig Party, Slesvigsk Parti, which represents the German minority in South Jutland, while Lolland Municipality has a mayor from the local party Din Stemme, Your Voice.

66
00:07:23.077 --> 00:07:30.912
As a result of the election to the four regional councils, three of the chairs come from Venstre and one from the Social Democrats.

67
00:07:31.462 --> 00:07:33.674
Finally, the organisations.

68
00:07:33.624 --> 00:07:38.687
The country's regions and municipalities have many tasks and interests in common.

69
00:07:38.687 --> 00:07:48.299
They therefore work closely together through Danske Regioner, Danish Regions, and KL, Kommunernes Landsforening, Local Government Denmark.

70
00:07:48.299 --> 00:07:54.687
The two organisations' overall goals are to strengthen local self-government in the regions and municipalities.

71
00:07:54.687 --> 00:08:02.826
For example, KL represents the municipalities in negotiations with the government about the municipalities' finances and about bills.

72
00:08:03.376 --> 00:08:05.726
Here is your recap for Episode Five.

73
00:08:05.676 --> 00:08:14.213
Local self-government is a cornerstone of Danish democracy, and the right of municipalities to manage their own affairs is in the constitution.

74
00:08:14.213 --> 00:08:20.238
The idea is the proximity principle: tasks should be solved as close to citizens as possible.

75
00:08:20.238 --> 00:08:23.963
Municipalities can levy tax; regions cannot.

76
00:08:23.963 --> 00:08:33.926
On the first of January, 2007, 271 municipalities became 98, and 14 counties became 5 regions.

77
00:08:33.926 --> 00:08:46.513
The five regions are the Capital Region with 29 municipalities, Zealand with 17, Southern Denmark with 22, Central Denmark with 19 and North Denmark with 11.

78
00:08:46.513 --> 00:08:53.163
Capital and Zealand merge on the first of January, 2027, into Region Østdanmark.

79
00:08:53.163 --> 00:08:56.426
Councils are elected for four years, in November.

80
00:08:56.426 --> 00:09:09.876
You can vote in local elections from eighteen, if you live permanently in the municipality and are either a Danish citizen, a citizen of an EU country, Iceland or Norway, or have lived in Denmark for the last four years.

81
00:09:09.876 --> 00:09:14.001
The last election was on the eighteenth of November, 2025.

82
00:09:14.001 --> 00:09:28.351
Mayors: Venstre 42, Social Democrats 26, Conservatives 20, SF 5, Liberal Alliance 2, Social Liberal Party 1, plus one from the Schleswig Party and one from Din Stemme.

83
00:09:28.351 --> 00:09:32.532
KL and Danske Regioner represent municipalities and regions.

84
00:09:33.082 --> 00:09:35.682
Now it is time for your exam practice.

85
00:09:35.632 --> 00:09:40.544
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

86
00:09:40.544 --> 00:09:48.030
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

87
00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:49.830
Question 1.

88
00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:52.405
How many municipalities are there in Denmark?

89
00:09:52.405 --> 00:09:55.418
Option A: 275.

90
00:09:55.418 --> 00:09:58.380
Option B: 179.

91
00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:00.360
Option C: 98.
[DA] Hvor mange kommuner er der i Danmark?  A: 275  |  B: 179  |  C: 98

92
00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:08.948
The answer is C: 98.

93
00:10:08.898 --> 00:10:13.170
It appeared in the November 2020 and June 2022 exams.
[DA] ✔ C: 98

94
00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:14.970
Question 2.

95
00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:16.720
When were the regions created?

96
00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:19.245
Option A: 2007.

97
00:10:19.245 --> 00:10:21.870
Option B: 1968.

98
00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:24.444
Option C: 1929.
[DA] Hvornår blev regionerne oprettet?  A: 2007  |  B: 1968  |  C: 1929

99
00:10:30.994 --> 00:10:33.494
The answer is A: 2007.

100
00:10:33.444 --> 00:10:37.782
It appeared in the November 2020 and November 2021 exams.
[DA] ✔ A: 2007

101
00:10:38.332 --> 00:10:39.670
Question 3.

102
00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:42.195
How often are municipal elections held?

103
00:10:42.195 --> 00:10:44.745
Option A: Every third year.

104
00:10:44.745 --> 00:10:47.170
Option B: Every fourth year.

105
00:10:47.170 --> 00:10:49.536
Option C: Every fifth year.
[DA] Hvor ofte afholdes der kommunalvalg?  A: Hvert 3. år  |  B: Hvert 4. år  |  C: Hvert 5. år

106
00:10:56.086 --> 00:10:58.799
The answer is B: Every fourth year.

107
00:10:58.749 --> 00:11:04.986
It appeared in the June 2021, November 2022 and November 2024 exams.
[DA] ✔ B: Hvert 4. år

108
00:11:05.536 --> 00:11:06.836
Question 4.

109
00:11:06.786 --> 00:11:08.711
Can the regions levy taxes?

110
00:11:08.711 --> 00:11:10.336
Option A: Yes.

111
00:11:10.336 --> 00:11:11.988
Option B: No.
[DA] Kan regionerne opkræve skatter?  A: Ja  |  B: Nej

112
00:11:18.538 --> 00:11:20.538
The answer is B: No.

113
00:11:20.488 --> 00:11:23.142
It appeared in the May 2023 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: Nej

114
00:11:23.692 --> 00:11:25.155
Question 5.

115
00:11:25.105 --> 00:11:27.267
Which authorities levy taxes?

116
00:11:27.267 --> 00:11:30.530
Option A: The state and the regions.

117
00:11:30.530 --> 00:11:34.217
Option B: The state and the municipalities.

118
00:11:34.217 --> 00:11:37.848
Option C: The regions and the municipalities.
[DA] Hvilke myndigheder udskriver skatter?  A: Staten og regionerne  |  B: Staten og kommunerne  |  C: Regionerne og kommunerne

119
00:11:44.398 --> 00:11:48.173
The answer is B: The state and the municipalities.

120
00:11:48.123 --> 00:11:51.090
It appeared in the November 2025 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: Staten og kommunerne

121
00:11:51.640 --> 00:11:53.078
Question 6.

122
00:11:53.028 --> 00:11:55.553
Who do municipal councils consist of?

123
00:11:55.553 --> 00:11:58.953
Option A: Civil servants appointed by the government.

124
00:11:58.953 --> 00:12:03.478
Option B: Politicians elected by the municipality's population.

125
00:12:03.478 --> 00:12:08.892
Option C: Representatives chosen by the local business and association life.
[DA] Hvem består kommunalbestyrelser af?  A: Embedsmænd udpeget af regeringen  |  B: Politikere valgt af kommunens befolkning  |  C: Repræsentanter udvalgt af det lokale erhvervs- og foreningsliv

126
00:12:15.442 --> 00:12:20.317
The answer is B: Politicians elected by the municipality's population.

127
00:12:20.267 --> 00:12:23.166
It appeared in the June 2026 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: Politikere valgt af kommunens befolkning

128
00:12:23.716 --> 00:12:25.479
That's the end of this part.

129
00:12:25.429 --> 00:12:26.496
Well done.

